+# Specialty Directory Assembled in a Read Model
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+`REQ-VET-003` adds a page listing every specialty with the veterinarians holding it. The stored association runs from `Vet` to `Specialty` and has no inverse. A specialty no veterinarian holds must still appear, so the page needs a specialty read path of its own.
+
+Every existing page renders persisted entities straight into a template. This page renders a grouping the store does not hold. [`architecture-principles.md`](../architecture-principles.md) bars a new business rule in a web controller, and the recorded controller deviation does not extend to new code.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Inverse mapping on `Specialty`** — add a `mappedBy` collection and render it directly.
+2. **Grouping in the controller or the template** — follow the codebase's straight-to-template convention.
+3. **Read model assembled from two repository reads** — a plain type turns veterinarians and specialties into the page's shape.
+
+## Decision
+
+We choose option 3. A new `SpecialtyRepository` reads every specialty, and the cached `VetRepository.findAll()` supplies the holders. A public read-model type in the `vet` package assembles the two into ordered entries, and a `SpecialtyController` serves them at the product-fixed address `/specialties.html`.
+
+Three constraints hold in the assembly:
+
+- Entries key on specialty identity, never on the `Specialty` object. Entities here inherit identity equality, and the two reads return separate instances of the same row.
+- Ordering happens in Java rather than in SQL. Specialties sort by name, holders by last name then first name, so the order is identical on all three database vendors.
+- The specialty read carries no `@Cacheable`. The one declared cache is `vets`, and a second no-argument cached read under that name collides on the shared empty key.
+
+## Consequences
+
+The grouping and its edge cases — an unheld specialty, a clinic with no specialty at all — become testable without the web layer. The `vet` package gains a repository, a read model, and a second controller.
+
+The repository interface exposes one read method, so the application still holds no write path to specialties, and NG-2 stands. The read model repeats ordering the store could supply, buying output that no vendor collation can change. The page costs two reads per request, one of them served from the vet cache; clinic-sized data keeps the in-memory join cheap. Option 1 remains available should a specialty ever need its holders away from this page.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-VET-003
+
+## References
+
+- [Feature-Package Organization Without a Service Layer](2026-07-31-feature-package-organization.md)
+- [Contracts in system-design.md](../system-design.md#contracts)
